[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good. Next: where it lives, which is really a question about what an attacker gets.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the persisted value is a [3mcredential[0m (something that proves who you are, so it must be unreadable by
  JavaScript) or a [3mlabel[0m (something that merely says who you are, safe for JS to read, and trusted by nobody).

  - [1mSession cookie for auth + userId in JS storage for the app[0m — the server sets an [38;5;153mHttpOnly; Secure; SameSite[39m cookie
    that carries the actual session; the userId your forms pass around is a plain non-secret label the server
    re-verifies on every request. Buys the standard split: XSS can read the label but can't steal the session. Costs a
    server that sets cookies — again, a contract we'd be designing against.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m only[0m — userId survives browser restart, available everywhere synchronously. Buys the simplest code by a
    wide margin. Costs durability in the wrong direction: it persists on shared machines until something explicitly
    clears it, and any XSS reads it. Fine for a label, dangerous the moment anything starts trusting it.
  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[39m only[0m — same API, scoped to the tab. Buys narrower exposure and automatic cleanup. Costs the "stay
    logged in" behavior people expect; a new tab is a fresh start.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the first, with [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m holding the userId label. The distinction that matters isn't the
  storage API, it's that the userId must never be the thing that authorizes anything — the server re-checks the session
  on every call, and the userId is convenience data. If we get that right, the storage choice becomes cheap to change
  later. If we get it wrong, every future form inherits a spoofable identity.

  Worth saying plainly: with [38;5;153mlogin()[39m a stub ([38;5;153mapp.js:4-8[39m) and no backend in this repo, whichever we pick, part of this
  design will be a documented contract rather than working auth.
